position = Vector2(-15, 0)

[node name="cheese_girl" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="5"]
editor_description = "The serving window, 20px right of the storefront's centre, where she is painted. Her presenter is placed here and left HIDDEN, because Actors Drawn Into Set names her. The mark exists so the schedule has somewhere to say she is and so the roster's left-to-right order stays true. Named for her actor id, which is how DepthEncounterController finds it and how an ordinary text search for the character finds her mark along with everything else about her."
position = Vector2(176, 0)

[node name="treasure_hunter" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="6"]
editor_description = "Between the shop and Rotini, 127px off the rat: (86.5 + 62.7) / 2 + 52.8. He stands nearest the cafe because he is the one who has stopped - 'No treasure here. Good. I was tired of carrying things.'"
position = Vector2(-466, 0)

[node name="rutini" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="7"]
editor_description = "On the miner's left, 110px off him: (52.8 + 62.7) / 2 + 52.8 measures 110.5. He arrived WITH the player - his colony has been digging alongside since 11,200 - so he is the only one here who is not waiting at the cafe but coming into it, and 'Easy, lads. We're here' is him calling them to a stop."
position = Vector2(-318, 0)

[node name="coffee_cat" type="Marker2D" parent="ActorMarkers" index="8"]
editor_description = "ON THE RIGHT-HAND STOOL, IN FRONT OF THE CAFE WINDOW. Zephan's layout: two stools stand at the serving window Cheese Girl is painted in, and the cat sits on the right one with the coffee he has been carrying since 9,200.

Y IS -32 AND IT IS A HEIGHT, NOT A POSITION. A gathered actor's mark is floor-sampled - the x says which column and the terrain says how high - so a negative y is added to the sampled ground rather than replacing it. Thirty two is the flat stool's seat, derived in local_tests/derive_flat_furniture.gd from a real 48cm stool, and it stays correct at every landing column because the rock under the stool is different rock each run and both read it from the same sampler."
position = Vector2(211, -32)

[node name="DasQuesoCafe" parent="PropMarkers" index="0" instance=ExtResource("5_cafe")]
editor_description = "RIGHT OF CENTRE, which is Zephan's layout. The door is on the building's left and the serving window Cheese Girl is painted in is on its right, both already in the art, so the shop faces back down the room toward everybody else.

It stands 40px clear of the Lantern Keeper's right edge - closer than the house spacing rule, deliberately, because a building is not a body and holding it a full miner-width off would push its own sign toward the frame edge.

SCALE AND THE ROOM ARE ONE DECISION. 0.10269 makes the 1626x1558 art 167px wide and 160px tall, twenty cells, still a third again the miner's own 120px. It was 24 cells and came down, because the frame is only 26 cells tall above the floor and this building tracks the miner across a 49-cell landing band - so whatever it needs is owed across seventy columns and the ceiling over all of them was pinned flat. local_tests/compare_room_ceilings.gd is the measure; encounter 3's cave, the one Zephan likes, swings from 1 cell to 36 because it protects no columns at all.

Y IS -16, AND THAT IS WHAT STOPS THE FRONT ROCK EATING IT.

Its base used to sit on the dig line - sixteen pixels UNDER the surface the cast are floor-sampled onto - so the floor band drew across its lower third and the shop could not be seen whole. It now stands on the same measured floor line the furniture uses. That was the whole fix; the z was a red herring.

Z -1, WHICH IS A NUMBER RATHER THAN A TIE. The room now orders strictly: storefront -1, its furniture 0, the cast 1, the foreground set and the front stratum 2.

It sat at 0, the same plane as its own stools, on the assumption that equal z falls back to tree order and index 0 draws first. It does not do so reliably for nodes added to an INHERITED scene, where the index is a hint rather than the child order - and the building drew over its own stools and table, which vanished. Quibble stayed visible only because the cast are a plane in front. Ordering that depends on tree position is ordering that breaks the next time somebody adds a prop. Re-run local_tests/carve_cafe_gathering_room.gd after changing this scale; it refuses to save rather than put the shop's roof in the stone."
z_index = -1
position = Vector2(159, -16)
scale = Vector2(0.10269, 0.10269)

[node name="CafeTableWindow" parent="PropMarkers" index="1" instance=ExtResource("14_table_flat")]
editor_description = "The cafe's own table, directly under the serving window, with a stool either side of it. Zephan's flat art: drawn side on, with no perspective, so it belongs to the same elevation the terrain and the characters are drawn in.

Y IS -16, MEASURED. A prop is not floor-sampled the way the cast are, so its y has to carry the whole correction itself - the level tunnel lays up to three cells of loose rock on the floor row. local_tests/capture_cafe_gathering_stage.gd samples the same floor the controller uses at every column this furniture can slide to and prints the number."
z_index = 0
position = Vector2(176, -16)

[node name="CafeStoolWindowLeft" parent="PropMarkers" index="2" instance=ExtResource("13_stool_flat")]
z_index = 0
position = Vector2(141, -16)

[node name="CafeStoolWindowRight" parent="PropMarkers" index="3" instance=ExtResource("13_stool_flat")]
editor_description = "Quibble's stool. ActorMarkers/coffee_cat sits on this one, 32px up, which is this stool's derived seat height."
z_index = 0
position = Vector2(211, -16)

[node name="ForegroundTable" parent="PropMarkers" index="4" instance=ExtResource("3_table")]
editor_description = "THE FOREGROUND SET, AND THE ONLY THING IN THIS ROOM ON LAYER ONE.

Zephan wants one table that is NOT flat - the old three-quarter drawing, which has a little depth in it - standing on the first stratum and sitting a touch lower on screen, so the cast read as walking behind it. That is the whole job: everything else in the shot is a flat elevation, and one prop with drawn depth in front of the cast is what gives the room a near and a far.

Z 2 is the foreground stratum's own draw order, one in front of the cast at z 1. Y IS +14, not the -16 the rest of the furniture uses: a foreground object sits LOWER in frame, and that offset is what sells it as nearer rather than as a table floating over the floor.

It stands to the left of the cafe, near the middle of the whole area, so it crosses the shot rather than decorating one end of it. It will overlap whoever is standing behind it - that is the point, and the stools are short enough that it is legs rather than faces.

STILL WAITING ON: Zephan asked for a shader on the first layer, like the road shader on the surface, so the flat area and the rocks there read as ground being walked on. That is terrain-wide and is NOT done here."
z_index = 2
position = Vector2(-136, 14)

[node name="ForegroundStoolLeft" parent="PropMarkers" index="5" instance=ExtResource("4_stool")]
z_index = 2
position = Vector2(-191, 14)

[node name="ForegroundStoolRight" parent="PropMarkers" index="6" instance=ExtResource("4_stool")]
z_index = 2
position = Vector2(-81, 14)

[node name="FarTableLeft" parent="PropMarkers" index="7" instance=ExtResource("14_table_flat")]
editor_description = "THE FAR RANK, WHICH IS WHERE THE 2.5D COMES FROM.

Zephan's note is that the room reads painfully flat and should sit closer to the surface's two-and-a-half D. The surface gets it from strata at different tints with a sky behind them; a tunnel has no sky, so this room gets it the way Rotini's colony already does. RatColonyFollowers ranks its mice by three values together - scale 1.0, 0.82, 0.66, 0.54; a tint darkening with depth; and a vertical offset that lifts further ranks UP the frame - and its own comment says the tint sells distance harder than the scale does.

This rank is that applied to furniture: 0.72 of the prop's OWN scale, darkened to 0.62, and lifted 34px, at z -2 behind everything including the storefront. Nobody sits at it, which is deliberate - a character on a 0.72 table would have to be scaled too, and the cast are shared presenters whose scale belongs to their own appearance.

AN INSTANCE SCALE REPLACES THE PROP SCENE'S SCALE, IT DOES NOT MULTIPLY IT. Writing 0.72 here drew a table 0.72 of its raw 2224px canvas - sixteen hundred pixels of furniture across the top of the shot. The numbers below are the prop's own scale times the rank factor, worked out rather than typed.

Move all three of these together or the plane breaks. Scale, tint and lift are one decision; changing one alone makes a table read as the wrong SIZE rather than as further away."
z_index = -2
modulate = Color(0.62, 0.6, 0.58, 1)
position = Vector2(-352, -34)
scale = Vector2(0.08286, 0.08286)

